<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constans II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/630">630</a>–<a href="/years/668">668</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Constantine the Bearded</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constans II was Byzantine emperor from 641 to 668, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a> that took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and threatened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. He moved his court west in 663, campaigned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a> in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and resided at Syracuse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, strengthening Byzantine positions there, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. He was murdered in his bath at Syracuse in 668.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constans-ii.jpg" alt="Constans II" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown; Image: Classical Numismatic Group, Inc. CNG allows the use of any of their images as long as they are attributed to CNG. [1] · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus_Constans_II_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Constans was born on 7 November 630 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, the son of Constantine III and Gregoria. Constantine III was a son of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heraclius">Heraclius</a>. When Heraclius died in February 641, Constantine III and his younger half-brother Heraclonas succeeded him, and Constantine III died suddenly after three months. In September 641 the ten-year-old Constans was crowned co-emperor, amid rumours that Heraclonas and his mother Martina had poisoned his father. Around 5 November the general Valentinus deposed Heraclonas, leaving Constans sole emperor. He reigned at first under a regency of senators led by Patriarch Paul II of Constantinople.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His reign coincided with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-muslim-conquests">Arab conquests</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> withdrew completely from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 642. A fleet under the admiral Manuel retook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 645, but its commander plundered the countryside and was forced to leave. In 647 Arab forces entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and Cappadocia, and a major offensive in 650 and 651 led Constans to negotiate with Mu'awiya I, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. In 655 Constans led a fleet against the Muslims at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-masts">Battle of the Masts</a>, off Lycia. About 500 Byzantine ships were destroyed and the emperor was almost killed. Peace with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> followed in 659, and in 658 he defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><p>In religion he tried to steer a middle line between Orthodoxy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monothelitism">Monothelitism</a>, forbidding discussion of the natures of Christ by the Typos of 648, which satisfied few. Pope Martin I condemned both the doctrine and the decree at the Lateran Council of 649. He was arrested in 653, brought to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, condemned and exiled to Cherson, where he died in 655. Constans also forced his younger brother Theodosius to take holy orders and had him killed in 660.</p><p>Having earned the hatred of the people of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, he left the city for Syracuse in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. From 663 he campaigned in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> against the Lombard Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Benevento</a>, besieging Benevento without success before withdrawing to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>. That year he spent twelve days in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, the first emperor to visit it since 476, where Pope Vitalian received him with honour. He stripped buildings, the Pantheon among them, of their bronze, and his later demands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calabria">Calabria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> enraged his Italian subjects.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 15 July 668 Constans was assassinated in his bath by his chamberlain, either with a bucket or with a knife. His son Constantine succeeded him as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-iv">Constantine IV</a> and quickly suppressed a brief usurpation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> by Mezezius. Chinese histories record an embassy from the Byzantine ruler in 643, which they name Boduoli, to the Tang emperor Taizong.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Constans II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even so, <b>Constans II</b> stripped its bronze roof tiles in 663, and the rotunda stayed uncovered for nearly a century until Pope Gregory III lined it with lead.</blockquote><a href="/work/pantheon">Pantheon · Survival &amp; Rediscovery</a></li><li><blockquote>Eugene I was chosen bishop of Rome after Emperor <b>Constans II</b> had Martin I arrested, carried off to Constantinople and banished to Cherson for his strong opposition to Monothelitism.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-10">10 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He kept a conciliatory relationship with Emperor <b>Constans II</b>, who visited him in Rome.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-30">30 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Pope Martin I, an opponent of Monothelitism, was arrested in the Lateran in Rome with Maximus the Confessor on the orders of Emperor <b>Constans II</b> and taken to Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-17">17 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 661 <b>Emperor Constans II</b> appointed Basil as its first duke, subject to the strategos of Sicily.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-naples">Duchy of Naples</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Emperor Constans II</b> had resided at Syracuse from 663 until his murder there in 668.</blockquote><a href="/state/theme-of-sicily">Theme of Sicily</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in Syracuse, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=654" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Constans II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=654">What was here in 654 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
